      "id": 1,
      "text": "Grayson, originally called Crossroads, was renamed in 1838 after Colonel Robert Grayson and officially incorporated as a city in 1860.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Grayson,_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The arrival of the Eastern Kentucky Railway in 1871 spurred Grayson's growth, linking the town to regional commerce.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Grayson,_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Grayson lies on the Little Sandy River, a tributary of the Ohio, which once powered early river‑based trade.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grayson%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The city experiences hot, humid summers and generally mild to cool winters, a typical climate for eastern Kentucky.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grayson%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Kentucky Christian University, formerly Kentucky Christian College, has its main campus in Grayson, offering a range of undergraduate programs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grayson%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
